<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice of Mudros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armistice of Mudros, signed on 30 October 1918 aboard HMS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> in Mudros harbour on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, ended fighting between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It opened the Dardanelles, demobilised the Ottoman army and allowed the Allies to occupy strategic points, leading to the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and other territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros.jpg" alt="Armistice of Mudros" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agamemnon_at_Mudros.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> advanced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> after the Russian Caucasus Army fell apart, while in Syria British forces pushed the Ottomans steadily back, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> fell in October. Enver Pasha, one of the most influential members of the government, hid information that made the position look weak and led most of the elite to believe the war was still winnable.</p><p>In September 1918 Allied forces under Louis Franchet d'Espèrey launched a sudden offensive on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-front">Macedonian front</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> sued for peace in the Armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> now faced defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> against an overland attack without Bulgarian help. Grand Vizier Talaat Pasha visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, concluded the war could not be won and persuaded his colleagues to resign. The ministry stepped down on 13 October, and Ahmed Izzet Pasha, the new Grand Vizier, sent the captured British General Charles Townshend to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> to seek terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The British cabinet decided to negotiate alone, excluding the French, and chose an armistice rather than a peace treaty, which they thought would need the approval of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and be too slow. Talks began on Sunday 27 October on the battleship HMS Agamemnon. The Ottoman delegation was led by Minister of Marine Affairs Rauf Bey, and the British refused to admit the senior French naval officer. Admiral Somerset Arthur Gough-Calthorpe held 24 demands but could concede any except occupation of the Dardanelles forts and free passage through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>.</p><p>Neither side knew the other was eager to settle, so the British draft was accepted largely unchanged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> surrendered their garrisons outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> the right to occupy forts on the Straits and any territory in case of disorder, demobilised the army, air force and navy, opened ports and railways, and withdrew to the pre-war borders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. It was signed on 30 October and took effect at noon the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> disliked the British acting alone, but accepted the matter as closed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> pointed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> armistice, which the French had concluded themselves.</p><p>Italian and Greek incursions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the name of restoring order soon came close to partition, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> of 1920 divided the empire into zones of influence. Nationalist forces based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> rejected it and took control of Anatolia by 1923. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a>, signed on 24 July 1923, superseded the armistice and set the borders of the Turkish state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armistice of Mudros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Armistice of Mudros</b> in 1918 and the Treaty of Sèvres of 1920 proposed the empire's partition, and the Allied occupation of Istanbul in 1918 and İzmir in 1919 set off the Turkish National Movement under Mustafa Kemal Pasha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Armistice of Mudros</b>, British and then French forces occupied Cilicia from late 1918, and in 1919 France took over full responsibility, administering it as part of the Occupied Enemy Territory Administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cilicia">French Cilicia</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Armistice of Mudros</b> in October 1918, the Ottoman Empire had to withdraw from Kars, Ardahan and Batum, lands it had gained by the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/southwestern-caucasian-republic">Southwestern Caucasian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
